TI  - Coral reef restoration and conservation in northeastern Brazil: Integrated actions in the face of marine heatwaves
T2  - MARINE POLLUTION BULLETIN
LA  - English
KW  - Marine heatwaves
KW  - Coral bleaching
KW  - Ecological restoration
KW  - Coastal management
KW  - Anthropogenic pressure
KW  - Climate resilience
AB  - The escalating degradation of coral reefs, driven primarily by anthropogenic activities and climate change, is one of the most serious environmental problems affecting the coastal zone of northeastern Brazil. This Perspective highlights the increasing frequency and severity of coral bleaching events linked to marine heatwaves and acidification. In recent years, bleaching episodes have become more frequent and separated by shorter intervals, jeopardizing the recovery and resilience of these ecosystems. The growing impacts of global warming in a region often overlooked in global bleaching assessments underscore the urgency of continuous and standardized monitoring to track changes and support effective coral reef conservation. Mitigation strategies-such as implementing coral nursery trees for ecological restoration and establishing multi-institutional partnerships for sustainable coastal management-represent essential actions for reef preservation. These strategies demonstrate effective measures to enhance coral ecosystem function and promote recovery, underscoring the need for urgent, multi-faceted approaches to coral reef conservation.
