AB  - Reef fish communities at thermal extremes are poorly documented but critical reference sites for monitoring responses to climate change. We surveyed Sir Bu Nair Island (SBN), a strictly enforced no-take marine protected area located 70 km offshore in the southern Arabian Gulf, the warmest sea on Earth. Visual surveys across 11 reef sites recorded a total of 57 fish species from 25 families revealing a diverse reef ecosystem of high conservation concern. Forty-two percent of fish species are IUCN Red Listed as Threatened and 32 % are priority Gulf fishery targets, including historically overexploited species. Predators comprised > 50 % of biomass, indicating an intact trophic pyramid. Threatened species, coral-dependent and heavily fished species collectively represented 90 % of total fish density, 75 % of total biomass, and 65 % of species richness. Mean fish density (similar to 319 individuals per 100 m(2)) and mean biomass (similar to 47 kg per 100 m(2)) at SBN exceed values reported for other reefs in the southern Gulf. Fish species richness and abundance increased with Acropora cover. Fish assemblage composition was similar between 5 m and 10 m depths, however, shallower reefs hosted more herbivores and predators, whereas deeper reefs supported more large and highly mobile species. SBN represents a rare area in the Arabian Gulf where coral specialists, high-value predators and branching corals persist together under chronic extreme heat. Protecting remaining Acropora stands and associated communities is crucial for sustaining biodiversity as marine heatwaves intensify.
