KW  - ISLANDS
KW  - SHIFTS
AB  - Monitoring seawater temperature is fundamental for understanding changes in marine biodiversity, especially in vertically stratified environments. We present water temperature variability analyses using low-cost sensors in the Alcatrazes Archipelago, Brazilian Southeastern region, showing strong seasonal vertical stratification. Temperature data was recorded at different depths at six sites between February/2022 and February/2024, complemented by thirty-five sampling campaigns using multiparameter probes. Metrics were derived from these observations, providing information about changes in thickness of the following categorical layers: surface mixed layer, thermocline, and contributions of the South Atlantic Central Water mass (SACW). Vertical stratification occurred between November and March, with a warm (29.0 degrees C) and shallow mixed layer and SACW reaching 14.4 degrees C at the bottom. The water column was vertically homogeneous from April to September, with 21.9 degrees C on average. However, winter temperatures differed notably, with 2023 being 1.1 degrees C warmer than 2022. SACW was consistently present in the summer, with estimated residence times up to 60 days at 25 m and 20 days at 10 m. In shorter time scales, spectral analysis revealed energy peaks from 2.4 to 7 days, suggesting that tides and the wind field disrupt the temperature vertical gradient, and a non-metric multidimensional scaling analysis clustered the sites according to their local depth. We verified the occurrence and persistence of warmer and colder water periods and the coincidence with NOAA's reports of marine heatwaves for a single site where data was available from December/2021 to May/2024. We found warmer events lasting 6.5 days on average at depths of 3 m and 8 m. The proposed simple metrics proved satisfactory for describing major vertical temperature gradient patterns in the region, subsidizing the understanding of the local biodiversity with depth and time. The approach can be replicated in different stratified sites in the coastal areas.
