AB  - Environmental changes affect the migration patterns of marine organisms, biotic interactions, and population dynamics, with temperature being a crucial factor influencing individual biological performance and community dynamics. Understanding how species respond to environmental variability is essential for predicting future ecosystem shifts under climate change. This study examines the response of Hypnea pseudomusciformis, a common intertidal red macroalga in Brazil, to different field and experimental conditions. Two morphotypes were studied: epilithic individuals exposed to desiccation and high temperatures, and epiphytic individuals submerged and epiphytic on Sargassum sp. Field data showed average air temperatures fluctuating between 19.9 degrees C in winter and 25.83 degrees C in summer, with notable cold fronts and heatwaves. In 2023, average biomass of the epilithic morphotype increased more than twice from May to September (1.73 to 5.58 g m(-2) DW), while the epiphytic population exhibited lower biomass (from 0.1 to 1.04 g m(-2) DW) throughout the year. Molecular analysis revealed no genetic differences between the morphotypes. Laboratory experiments indicated that epiphytic individuals had higher growth rates and photosynthetic efficiencies at intermediate temperatures (20-28 degrees C) compared to epilithic individuals. The epiphytic morphotype experienced greater water loss during desiccation, highlighting its vulnerability. These physiological differences suggest that morphotypes show different range of responses to environmental conditions. Winter temperatures were optimal for H. pseudomusciformis reproduction and growth, while summer temperatures imposed physiological stress. This study highlights the importance of phenotypic plasticity in H. pseudomusciformis' responses to environmental variability, as seen in the distinct physiological and ecological traits of the two morphotypes. The ability of this species to exhibit different growth patterns, photosynthetic efficiencies, and desiccation tolerances enhances its resilience to environmental disturbances, reinforcing the role of plasticity as a key mechanism for survival and adaptation in changing marine ecosystems.
